<header><p class="enc-kicker">People / group</p><h1 class="enc-title">House of Oldenburg</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The House of Oldenburg is a European royal dynasty descended from the medieval counts of Oldenburg in north-western <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/germany">Germany</a>. In 1448 Count Christian of Oldenburg was elected King of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/denmark">Denmark</a> as Christian I, and his descendants ruled Denmark, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/norway">Norway</a> and, for periods, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sweden">Sweden</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/schleswig-holstein">Schleswig-Holstein</a> and Oldenburg itself. Its branches include the Holstein-Gottorp line, which gave rulers to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/russia">Russia</a> and Sweden, and the Glücksburg line, which still reigns in Denmark and Norway.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/house-of-oldenburg.jpg" alt="House of Oldenburg" width="320" height="375" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Arms_of_the_County_of_Oldenburg.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>Count Elimar I of Oldenburg was first mentioned in 1091, and the family's ancestral home is Oldenburg Castle. In the 12th century Rastede Monastery near Oldenburg became their house monastery. Marriages of the medieval counts gave the family a route to the Scandinavian thrones: through marriage with a descendant of King Valdemar I of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sweden">Sweden</a> and King <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/eric-iv-of-denmark">Eric IV of Denmark</a>, the family staked a claim to Sweden and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/denmark">Denmark</a> as early as 1350.</p><p>In the time of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/margaret-i-of-denmark">Margaret I</a>, queen of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/denmark">Denmark</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sweden">Sweden</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/norway">Norway</a>, the chief competitor for the northern thrones was the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/house-of-mecklenburg">House of Mecklenburg</a>, and the Duke of Saxe-Lauenburg was among other aspirants. In 1423 Theodoric of Oldenburg married Hedwig of Holstein, a descendant of Magnus Ladulås of Sweden, Haakon V of Norway and the Danish kings Eric V and Abel.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>Their son Christian became King of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/denmark">Denmark</a> in 1448, of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/norway">Norway</a> in 1450 and of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sweden">Sweden</a> in 1457, ruling the whole <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kalmar-union">Kalmar Union</a>. The main line then ruled Denmark until 1863 and Norway until 1814, and held Sweden in 1457–1464, 1497–1501 and 1520–1521. The kings were also dukes of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/schleswig">Schleswig</a> from 1460 and counts, later dukes, of Holstein. The old comital line of Oldenburg itself became extinct in 1667.</p><p>The family divided into branches. The Holstein-Gottorp line, which held the duchy of Holstein-Gottorp from 1544 to 1739, gave kings to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sweden">Sweden</a> between 1751 and 1818, and for a time to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/norway">Norway</a> in 1814–1818. Its branch commonly still called <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/house-of-romanov">Romanov</a> provided emperors of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/russia">Russia</a> in 1762 and from 1796 to 1917, and another Holstein-Gottorp line ruled as dukes and grand dukes of Oldenburg from 1774 to 1918. The Glücksburg branch, whose name changed from Schleswig-Holstein-Sonderburg-Beck in 1825, succeeded to the Danish throne in 1863.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Legacy</h2><p>The Glücksburg kings also ruled elsewhere: they were kings of the Hellenes from 1863 to 1924 and from 1935 to 1973, kings of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/norway">Norway</a> from 1905, and kings of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iceland">Iceland</a> from 1918 to 1944. The Augustenburg branch, extinct in 1931, claimed the dukedom of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/schleswig-holstein">Schleswig-Holstein</a> in 1863. The line of Elimar I is also traced in the male line to the present kings of Norway and the United Kingdom, and the Danish royal house is a member of the family through descent that is not strictly male. Illegitimate offshoots took the name Danneskiold.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “House of Oldenburg” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Christian I, elected king of Denmark in 1448 and the first king of the <b>House of Oldenburg</b>, was crowned in Copenhagen Cathedral.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/october-28">28 October in history</a></li><li><blockquote>In 1448 Count Christian of Oldenburg was elected King of Denmark as Christian I, founding the <b>House of Oldenburg</b>, which later ruled Denmark, Norway, Sweden, Russia and Greece in its various branches.</blockquote><a href="/state/county-of-oldenburg">County of Oldenburg</a></li></ul></section>
